Cat lifecycle latency on plain bare metal, both block paths.

host:     AWS c5.metal, 96 cpus, 188 GiB -- NO instance store, so the ChunkStore
          cache and the file object store both sit on the EBS gp3 root
          (6000 IOPS / 500 MB/s provisioned). This is the "ordinary bare metal"
          shape, as opposed to i4i.metal's local NVMe.
date_utc: 2026-07-27
raw:      c5-lifecycle.log

--- single Cat: the two paths are indistinguishable to a user -----------------

Median of 3, ms. The first create on a node is excluded from the median: it
publishes the golden image once (6192 ms kernel-nbd / 5891 ms vhost-user).

  operation                 kernel-nbd   vhost-user
  create (cold)                   1681         1827
  warm (snapshot + VMM exit)       465          464
  restore (from snapshot)          163          163
  suspend (vm.pause)               162          163
  resume (vm.resume)               163          162
  stop                             163          162
  start (cold, after stop)        1674         1674

Create and cold start land on the same number (~1.7 s), which is what should
happen once golden roots mean neither uploads any data: both are dominated by
booting a VM. Restore at 163 ms is 3x cheaper than warm at 465 ms -- storing the
snapshot is the expensive half, which is the shape the Warm state wants.

--- but the node agent's own telemetry is NOT indistinguishable ---------------

Warm restore, internal phase timings (µs), 3 samples each:

  path         total   blockd   block_attach   api_ready   resume   guest_health
  kernel-nbd   93705    11047          37311       41456     1544          1776
  kernel-nbd   92818    11150          32371       44516     1464          2827
  kernel-nbd   90486    10843          36986       39521     1241          1551
  vhost-user   35307    11216              3       21368     1229          1127
  vhost-user   39750    11256              4       25418     1531          1146
  vhost-user   38391    11266              5       23355     1427          1912

  mean total:         92.3 ms  ->  37.8 ms      2.4x faster
  mean block attach:  35.6 ms  ->  4 µs         and it was 38% of the restore

The user-visible restore is 163 ms on both paths because ~125 ms of control-plane
round trip sits on top and swamps the difference. So the block path advantage is
real and measurable, but today it is hidden end-to-end by overhead above it.

--- concurrency is where they separate ----------------------------------------

concurrent-start.sh, 0 failures in every cell. min / p50 / max ms.

  create        kernel-nbd                    vhost-user
       1        1679 / 1679 / 1679            1828 / 1828 / 1828
       4        1853 / 5008 / 6670            1826 / 3665 / 3665
      10        1823 / 8236 / 16692           1822 / 3873 / 3874

  restore       kernel-nbd                    vhost-user
       1         162 /  162 /  162             162 /  162 /  162
       4         162 /  330 /  467             178 /  182 /  182
      10         207 /  642 / 1129             218 /  235 /  371

  warm          kernel-nbd                    vhost-user
       1         466 /  466 /  466             463 /  463 /  463
       4        2122 / 2584 / 2735            2577 / 2903 / 3053
      10        6051 / 14260 / 14713          6218 / 14085 / 14394

* 10 concurrent creates: 16692 ms on kernel-nbd against 3874 ms on vhost-user,
  4.3x. And look at the shape: vhost-user's p50 at n=10 (3873 ms) is barely above
  its single-Cat time, while kernel-nbd's climbs to 8236 ms with a 16.7 s tail.
  That is the node-wide /dev/nbdN allocation lock, which the node agent takes for
  every kernel-nbd launch and never for vhost-user.
* 10 concurrent restores: 1129 ms against 371 ms, 3.0x, and far tighter
  (p50 642 vs 235).
* Warm is the same on both paths, which fits: writing a snapshot is not
  block-path work.

--- what this adds to the default-path decision ------------------------------

Single Cat: no user-visible difference. Concurrency: vhost-user is 3-4x better on
both create and wake. Combined with the fio result from i4i (vhost-user wins all
six workloads once the host page cache is out of the comparison) and with the
robustness finding below, the case for vhost-user no longer rests on throughput
alone.

Robustness, observed on i4i earlier the same day, not a measurement but worth
recording: when a cats-blockd dies with its /dev/nbdN still attached, any process
touching that device enters uninterruptible sleep. The node agent became
unkillable -- it survived SIGKILL -- and kept port 7301 bound, so systemd could
not restart it and every later phase failed. Recovery needed a manual
`nbd-client -d`. The vhost-user path has no kernel device to orphan.
